Motion Capture-Based Arch Height Index SOP

This SOP assumes that you have collected motion capture data of a human participant and used a reflective marker set that includes that following markers: 

- Posterior Calcaneus
- Lateral Calcaneus
- Proximal Base of the 2nd Metatarsal
- Distal Head of the 2nd Metatarsal
- Distal Head of the 1st Metatarsal


1) Open Visual 3D
2) Model > Create > Hybrid Model From .c3d File > Select Static Trial
3) Model > Apply Model Template > Select Model Template (Download from repository)
4) Subject Data/Metrics > Edit Marker_Radius (Enter your reflective marker radius) > Edit Mass and Height (Enter participant values)
5) Build Model
6) File > Open/Add > Insert New Files Into Your Currently Open Workspace > Select current static trial again and any additional motion trials to want to analyze
    Filename Error will appear > Click "Append Suffix" (now you will have a static trial and a static copy trial, alternatively you can use a different static stranding trial than the one used to build the model)
7) Model > Assign Model to Motion Files → Select all trials (including the static trial) → Okay
8) Signals and Events > Select static trial from drop down menu > Run Pipeline 1 
* Only after running Pipeline 1 *
9) Signals and Events > Ensure "All Files" is selected in drop down menu > Run Pipeline 2
    Please note that the processing pipeline subtracts a marker radius of 0.00475 from dorsum height and truncated foot length. Please update the pipeline to use the appropriate marker radius for your markers. 

At this point the results including the dorsum height, truncated foot length, and arch height index during the trials will be saved to a .txt file for each foot.


Arch deformation during motion trials can be calculated from the data by using the following equation: 

Arch deformation(t) = Dynamic AHI(t) - Dynamic AHI(t0) 
